{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfaDRvgPWSZwyVTtiuY39","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate for round 4, slot 1: **`serial_position_weighted_alternation`**. The self-check prints `OK`. That check does not test novelty against the other models, and a single chain can't verify convergence.\n\n**Hypothesis:** people pay more attention to the start and end of a sequence than to its middle (a serial-position effect). So the switches and repeats between neighbouring flips at the edges count for more when they judge how often a sequence switches. Each person compares this edge-weighted switching rate with their own ideal switching rate and picks the sequence that comes closer. A streak at the start or end therefore looks less random than the same streak in the middle. This targets the top critique finding: among pairs with similar alternation rates, people avoided the sequence with an edge streak (0.608 observed vs 0.529 predicted, p=0.03, q=0.12). No current model uses where in a sequence a flip falls.\n\n**How it's built:** the edge weighting is one shared parameter, `kappa`, with a Normal(0, 1) prior. It sets how much more an edge transition counts than a middle one, and a negative value would mean the middle counts more. The personal ideal switching rate is hierarchical and non-centred, as in `personal_ideal_alternation`. There is one sensitivity parameter, `beta`. The model has no balance term and no guessing rate: this is an exploratory slot, which bars grafting components from other models.\n\n**Smoke-fit results:**\n- **ELPD-LOO:** -1002.4. That beats `personal_ideal_alternation` (-1009), the model it reduces to when `kappa` = 0, but it is still behind the best model (-952.4). This came from 100 draws on one chain, so treat it as a rough indication only.\n- **Novelty risk:** if `kappa`'s posterior sits near 0, the model predicts almost the same as `personal_ideal_alternation`, and the novelty check could reject it as a near-duplicate.\n- **PSIS-LOO reliability:** 11.1% of trials had Pareto k above 0.5, against a 1% tolerance. On this short fit that makes the ELPD estimate itself unreliable; the full admission fit will show whether that persists.\n\nThe files are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run1/data/subjective_randomness/experiment1/model_loop/iter_4/candidate_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3011,"cache_read_input_tokens":61618,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3011,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c482d75d-e4a4-451d-b2e4-6b05ed585da6","uuid":"41939b56-2aae-4167-b573-ea1876065400","timestamp":"2026-09-30T19:58:11.301Z","request_id":"req_011CfaDRufNkVSfAS1Z9nKQk"}
